.\"+++
.\"
.\"	FACILITY:	SEARCH - OpenVMS-style record search utility for Linux
.\"
.\"	MODULE:		doc/search.1
.\"
.\"	ABSTRACT:	The manual page of the SEARCH utility.
.\"
.\"	AUTHOR:		StarLet Squad and Ruslan R. Laishev (AKA: BadAss SysMan)
.\"
.\"	CREATION DATE:	 1-AUG-2026
.\"
.\"	MODIFICATION HISTORY:
.\"
.\"		 1-AUG-2026	RRL	X-01 : Initial version.
.\"
.\"---
.TH SEARCH 1 "4 August 2026" "SEARCH @SEARCH_IDENT@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
search \- display the records of a set of files that carry given strings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B search
.I file-spec\fR[,...]
.I search-string\fR[,...]
.RI [ /qualifiers ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B search
reads the records of every selected file and displays those that satisfy a
boolean combination of the given search strings, after the DCL
.B SEARCH
command of OpenVMS.
.PP
A search string is a plain
.B substring
\- no wildcard, no regular expression, nothing to escape.  A file
specification, in contrast,
.B is
expanded by the utility itself, so quote it against the shell to have it
expanded here, which is what
.B /EXCLUDE
and
.B /RECURSIVE
need to be of any use.
.PP
The first word that is not a qualifier is the list of the file
specifications; every word after it is a list of the search strings.  A
list is separated by commas; an element carrying a comma of its own is to
be enclosed in double quotes, or passed as a word of its own.
.PP
A qualifier may be abbreviated as long as the abbreviation is unambiguous,
and may be spelled in the Unix long form:
.B \-\-EXACT
for
.BR /EXACT .
A single dash begins an ordinary value, so a file spelled
.I \-notes.txt
is asked for as it stands;
.B \-h
alone is the short alias of
.BR /HELP .
.PP
A word beginning with a slash is taken for a qualifier only when it names
one:
.B /RECURSIVE
does,
.I /usr/src/*.c
and
.I /etc
do not, since a slash begins an absolute file specification here as well.
A word that names no qualifier is a parameter, so a misspelt qualifier
becomes one instead of being refused; spelled in the long form,
.BR \-\-NAME ,
it is a qualifier beyond doubt and the misspelling is reported.  A lone
.B \-\-
ends the qualifiers, so that a file or a string spelled like one can be
asked for.
.SH QUALIFIERS
.TP
.B /EXACT
The case of the letters is significant.  By default it is ignored; a byte
above 0x7f is always compared as it stands, so a Cyrillic string is matched
exactly as it has been typed either way.
.TP
.BI /MATCH= option
How the search strings are combined within a record:
.B OR
(the default) \- at least one is present;
.B AND
\- every one is present;
.B NAND
\- at least one is absent;
.B NOR
\- none is present.
.TP
.BI /WINDOW [=( n , m )]
Display
.I n
records before and
.I m
records after a match.  One value means that many on either side, no value
means one on either side.  Overlapping windows produce one continuous run
of records \- no record is displayed twice \- and a gap between two runs is
marked by an empty line.
.TP
.B /[NO]PAGE
Stop the display at the end of every page and wait.  An empty line
displays the next page; an answer beginning with
.B Q
abandons the display, and the files still to be searched are not opened
at all.
.IP
Paging is granted only when the output really is a terminal and an
answer can be read from one -- from the standard input when that is a
terminal, from
.I /dev/tty
otherwise -- so a redirected run never waits for a keystroke.  The
height of the page is the one the terminal driver reports.  Off by
default.
.TP
.BI /[NO]HIGHLIGHT [=style]
Mark, within every displayed record, the runs that belong to a search
string.  The style is
.BR BOLD " (the default), " REVERSE ", " UNDERLINE ", " RED ", " GREEN
or
.BR YELLOW .
.IP
Overlapping occurrences are marked as one run.  A record displayed as
part of the window of
.B /WINDOW
carries no match of its own and is never marked.
.IP
The marking uses the escape sequences of ECMA-48 and is therefore
granted by default only when the output is a terminal;
.B /HIGHLIGHT
given outright overrides that,
.B /NOHIGHLIGHT
suppresses the marking whatever the output is.
.TP
.B /[NO]NUMBERS
Prefix, or do not prefix, the number of the record.  Off by default.
.TP
.B /[NO]HEADING
Announce, or do not announce, every file before its first matching
record.  The heading is a rule of asterisks, the specification of the
file and its time stamp -- the very stamp the selection works on, so
that a run with
.B /SINCE
displays the time it has compared against.  On by default.
.TP
.BI /OUTPUT= filespec
Write the result to a file instead of the standard output.  The
diagnostics keep going to the standard error stream.
.TP
.B /[NO]LOG
Report every file searched, with the number of records read and matched,
and account, before the first file is opened, for what the command has
been made to mean:
.IP
.RS
.nf
%SEARCH\-I\-SEARCHING, for "alpha", "beta" combined by AND, ...
%SEARCH\-I\-SELECTING, in *.c, /RECURSIVE, /EXCLUDE=(build/%*)
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B /[NO]STATISTICS
Report the totals at the end of the run.
.TP
.BI /EXCLUDE= (pattern,...)
Drop the files matching a pattern.  A pattern that carries a slash is
matched against the whole specification of a file, one that does not \-
against its name alone.  An excluded directory is not descended into at
all.
.TP
.BI /SINCE= time "\fR, \fB/BEFORE=" time
Take the files that are not older than, or that are older than, the given
time.  See
.B TIME
below.  A delta and a keyword say nothing about the date they land on, so
.B /LOG
reports what the value resolved to:
.IP
.RS
.nf
%SEARCH\-I\-SELTIME, /SINCE=0\-1:00 is  1\-AUG\-2026 13:52:20.00
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B /MODIFIED\fR, \fB/CREATED
Which time stamp
.B /SINCE
and
.B /BEFORE
apply to: the time of the last modification (the default), or the time of
the last change of the inode \- the closest analogue Linux has to a
creation time.  The two cannot be given together.
.TP
.B /RECURSIVE
Descend into the subdirectories \- the analogue of the
.B [...]
of an OpenVMS directory specification.  A symbolic link is never followed,
neither to a file nor to a directory.
.TP
.BI /BINARY= option
What is done with a binary file, one that carries a NUL among its first
32 KB:
.B NOTE
(the default) says in one line that it matches,
.B SKIP
leaves it alone,
.B TEXT
searches and displays it as any other file.
.TP
.BI /FORMAT= option
.B VMS
(the default) displays headings and records;
.B GREP
displays every record as
.IR spec : number : record ,
one line each, as
.B grep \-n
does, for a tool to read: no heading, every record numbered, a record of
the window parted by hyphens, two runs parted by a line \(lq\-\-\(rq.
.TP
.BI /HELP " [topic]"
Display the description of the utility -- the help library of the
product -- and stop.  The words that follow name a topic within it:
.B "search /HELP window"
is the description of
.BR /WINDOW ,
a word that names no subtopic being tried once more with a slash in
front of it.
.IP
The library is looked for where
.B SEARCH$HELPLIB
says, then beside the image, then where the product was configured to
install it; failing all of them, and in a build made without the HELP
product at hand, a summary of the qualifiers built into the image
answers instead.
.SH WILDCARDS
A file specification is expanded by the utility:
.B *
matches any run of characters,
.B %
and
.B ?
match exactly one.  The wildcards apply to the name within a directory:
the part of the specification up to the last slash is a directory and is
not expanded \- walk a tree with
.B /RECURSIVE
instead.  A specification carrying no wildcard is taken literally: a
directory stands for everything in it, a file stands for itself.
.SH TIME
A time value is read the way
.B UTIL$BINTIM
of the STARLET module reads it, plus the keywords
.BR TODAY ,
.BR YESTERDAY ,
.B TOMORROW
and
.BR NOW :
.PP
.RS
.nf
/SINCE=1\-AUG\-2026        an absolute date
/SINCE=14:30             today at half past two
/SINCE=0\-1:00            a delta: within the last hour
/SINCE=TODAY             since midnight
.fi
.RE
.SH RECORDS
A record is a line.  The last line of a file that ends without a
terminator is a record none the less.  A record is searched and displayed
whole however long it is; only a record longer than 64 MB in a file that
cannot be mapped (a pipe, a device) is cut, which affects the display
.I and
the search, and is therefore announced by
.BR %SEARCH\-W\-LONGREC .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one record matched.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing matched, and nothing went wrong.  Nothing matched at all is reported
by
.BR %SEARCH\-W\-NOMATCHES .
.TP
.B 2
An error was signalled \- a file that could not be opened or read, a
command that could not be carried out \- whatever else has matched.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B search '*.txt' error
Every record of every .TXT file of the current directory that carries the
string, the case being ignored.
.TP
.B search '*.c' alpha,beta /MATCH=AND /NUMBERS
The records that carry both strings, prefixed by their numbers.
.TP
.B search '*' STS$K_ /RECURSIVE /EXCLUDE=(build/%*,*.o)
The whole tree below the current directory, one directory and every object
file left out.
.TP
.B search 'src/*.c' TODO "/WINDOW=(1,2)" /OUTPUT=todo.lis
Every match with one record before and two after it, written to a file.
.TP
.B search '*.c' alpha /RECURSIVE /PAGE /HIGHLIGHT=REVERSE
The whole tree, a page at a time, every occurrence in reverse video.
.TP
.B search \-\- notes.txt /EXACT
The string /EXACT in the file, rather than the qualifier.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The diagnostics are signalled by the message subsystem of the STARLET
library, on the standard error stream.  A record carries the date, the
time and the thread, and then the condition in the OpenVMS format:
.PP
.RS
.nf
14\-08\-2026 12:25:29.279    841 %SEARCH\-W\-NOFILES, no file matches *.lis
.fi
.RE
.PP
A build made for debugging names the module, the routine and the line as
well.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1),
.BR vhelp (1)
.PP
The fuller description of the utility is a help library of the HELP
subsystem, installed as
.IR <prefix>/share/help/search.hlb :
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ vhelp search
$ vhelp \-\- search /window
.fi
.RE
.PP
The DCL SEARCH command of OpenVMS, of which this utility is the portable
analogue.
.SH AUTHOR
StarLet Squad and Ruslan R. Laishev (AKA: BadAss SysMan).
